<title>UK: UK Labour's Brown - EMU would not end fiscal freedom.</title>
<headline>UK Labour's Brown - EMU would not end fiscal freedom.</headline>
<dateline>LONDON 1996-12-11</dateline>
<text>
<p>Gordon Brown, finance spokesman for Britain's opposition Labour party, said participation in a single European currency would not end Britain's freeedom to set its own fiscal policies.</p>
<p>&quot;I share the same view as the chancellor on this matter -- I do not accept that that removes our freedom to make decisions on taxation and other issues in this House of Commons,&quot; he said during a parliamentary debate on Europe.  </p>
<p>Brown said there could be substantial benefits in a single currency but a Labour government would decide whether or not to join in the country's interest at the appropriate time.</p>
<p>&quot;We must make a decision when we know the full economic information and can make an assessment of it,&quot; he said.</p>
<p>&quot;We do not accept you move to a federal Europe immediately you look at the issue of a single currency,&quot; he added. &quot;We will look at the issue in terms of the national interest.&quot;</p>
<p>-- Richard Murphy, London Newsroom 44 171 542 2774</p>
